.Dd November 3, 2026
.Dt ZKCMP 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm zkcmp
.Nd zero-knowledge file and password comparison utility
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl h Ar hash
.Op Fl s Ar salt
.Fl c Ar file
.Fl o Ar commit
.Nm
.Op Fl h Ar hash
.Op Fl s Ar salt
.Fl p Ar file
.Op Fl t Ar seconds
.Fl o Ar proof
.Nm
.Op Fl h Ar hash
.Op Fl s Ar salt
.Fl C
.Fl o Ar commit
.Nm
.Op Fl h Ar hash
.Op Fl s Ar salt
.Fl P
.Op Fl t Ar seconds
.Fl o Ar proof
.Nm
.Fl g Ar salt
.Nm
.Op Fl q
.Op Fl h Ar hash
.Op Fl t Ar seconds
.Ar commit
.Ar proof
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility provides zero-knowledge proof and comparison operations for files and
passwords.
It allows a prover to demonstrate knowledge of a secret derived from a file or
a password, without revealing the secret or the file, and without sending the
password.
.Pp
A commitment is a public value derived from the secret.
It may be published once and used to verify any number of independent proofs.
Commitments and proofs are written as binary files.
.Pp
The commands are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl c Ar file
Create a public commitment from
.Ar file
and write it to the file named by
.Fl o .
.It Fl p Ar file
Create a zero-knowledge proof from
.Ar file
and write it to the file named by
.Fl o .
.It Fl C
Like
.Fl c ,
but the secret is a password read from the terminal.
The password is requested twice.
.It Fl P
Like
.Fl p ,
but the secret is a password read from the terminal.
The password is requested once.
.It Fl g Ar salt
Generate a random 32 byte private salt and write it to
.Ar salt .
The file is created with mode 0600.
.It Ar commit Ar proof
Verify
.Ar proof
against
.Ar commit .
.El
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl h Ar hash
Select the hash algorithm used to derive the secret and the challenge.
.Ar hash
is one of
.Cm sha256
(the default),
.Cm sha3-256
or
.Cm sha512-256 .
The same algorithm must be used for commitment, proof and verification.
.It Fl o Ar file
Write the commitment or proof to
.Ar file .
Required by
.Fl c ,
.Fl p ,
.Fl C
and
.Fl P .
.It Fl q
Quiet mode.
Do not print a message when verification fails; report the result through the
exit status only.
.It Fl s Ar salt
Use the private salt in
.Ar salt .
With
.Fl c
and
.Fl p
the salt is hashed together with the file.
With
.Fl C
and
.Fl P
it is used as the salt of the password key derivation function.
The same salt must be given when creating the commitment and the proof.
It is not needed for verification.
.It Fl t Ar seconds
Bind the proof to a time window of
.Ar seconds
seconds.
With
.Fl p
and
.Fl P ,
the clock is divided into windows of that length, and the index of the window
in which the proof is created is hashed into the challenge.
The proof is accepted during its own window and the following one, so it stays
valid for at least
.Ar seconds
and at most twice that.
With verification,
.Ar seconds
must be the same value given to the prover.
A proof made with
.Fl t
does not verify without it, and a proof made without it does not verify with it.
.Fl t
is not accepted with
.Fl c ,
.Fl C
or
.Fl g .
.El
.Pp
On
.Ox ,
.Nm
uses
.Xr pledge 2
and gives up all file system access once its inputs have been read.
.Sh PROTOCOL
The zero-knowledge protocol is a Schnorr-style proof of knowledge using the
3072-bit MODP group defined by RFC 3526.
The proof uses a Fiat-Shamir challenge, which makes verification
non-interactive.
The commitment is Y = g^x mod p, where x is the secret value reduced modulo
the group order.
The challenge is c = H(Y || R [|| window]) mod q, where R = g^k mod p for a
random k, H is the selected hash, and the window is included only when
.Fl t
is used.
The response is s = k + c*x mod q.
The verifier accepts when g^s == R * Y^c mod p.
.Pp
For
.Fl c
and
.Fl p ,
the selected hash is computed over a domain separator, the optional
private salt and the contents of the file.
The resulting digest is the secret value of the proof.
.Pp
For
.Fl C
and
.Fl P ,
the password is stretched with PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA512 using 600000 iterations and
the salt.
The derived key is then hashed with the domain separator using the selected
hash to produce the secret value.
The key derivation does not depend on
.Fl h ,
so password commitments are interchangeable between systems.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It 0
The operation completed or the proof verified successfully.
.It 1
The proof did not match the commitment, or the proof has expired.
.It 2
An error occurred.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Create a commitment for a file, and a proof of knowledge of it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ zkcmp -c file -o commit
$ zkcmp -p file -o proof
$ zkcmp commit proof
.Ed
.Pp
Publish a commitment once and let several parties prove knowledge
independently:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
server$ zkcmp -c release.tar -o release.commit
alice$  zkcmp -p release.tar -o alice.proof
bob$    zkcmp -p release.tar -o bob.proof
server$ zkcmp release.commit alice.proof
server$ zkcmp release.commit bob.proof
.Ed
.Pp
Create a proof valid for at least one hour, and verify it within that time:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
prover$   zkcmp -p file -t 3600 -o proof
server$   zkcmp -t 3600 commit proof
.Ed
.Pp
Use a private salt so that the commitment cannot be checked against guessed
files:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ zkcmp -g shared.salt
$ zkcmp -s shared.salt -c file -o commit
$ zkcmp -s shared.salt -p file -o proof
.Ed
.Pp
Register and authenticate a password, using a per-user salt:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ zkcmp -g user.salt
$ zkcmp -s user.salt -C -o user.commit
$ zkcmp -s user.salt -P -o user.proof
$ zkcmp user.commit user.proof
.Ed
.Pp
Use SHA3-256 and check the result in a script:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ zkcmp -h sha3-256 -p file -o proof
$ zkcmp -q -h sha3-256 commit proof && echo ok
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr cmp 1 ,
.Xr pledge 2
